A||||8||Stoic||Fiero gt vs. RX7?||||5||
Z||000000||Stoic||12-09-2000||03:47 PM||||Just wondering how a fiero gt would match up against a 89-90 RX7 (non-turbo)?||||reg||5
Z||000001||AkursedX||12-09-2000||04:17 PM||||Well the non-turbo RX-7 has 160hp/140ft/lbs of torque.  They have comperable weights, and handling capabilities.  I think the Fiero would win over short distances, but the RX-7 would win out in the top end. <BR>So I think in a 1/4, it would be close, but I would go with the Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000002||pollock||12-09-2000||06:46 PM||||I had a gen2 RX7 before my fiero, and i can tell you it will be damn close.<P>DO NOT expect to get a holeshot on the RX7 like we can against all other cars.  They're nasty off the line.  My standard procedure was to hold it at 3000rpm and step off the clutch.  The ass would drop and it would go like hell.  <P>I'd like to see that race.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||horseman||12-10-2000||08:01 PM||||Has anyone checked the weight / horsepower ratio? I think the RX-7 has an advantage but I am not sure. Having owned a second gen '88 7, I would think that it could handle most stock GT's. The turbo cars especially should run away from them. I loved my 7 but I wanted to get back to an american daily driver. You know; U.S. pride,cheaper parts, easier to service etc. Besides, the Fiero is a Pontiac and I always loved the divisions styling and sportiness. I also own a mint '66 GTO convertible(64,000 mi), and a '79 Trans Am(400,4spd,4 wheel disk brakes), and a '92 Formula(305 h.o, auto) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Charlie||||reg||1
Z||000004||yobtah||12-10-2000||11:48 PM||||I'm assuming you're thinking about drag racing... I had a 2nd gen. RX-7 before my '87 SE V6 Fiero.  I think the RX-7 would probably be slightly faster for a few reasons.<P>The rotary revs much easier than the 2.8.  Throttle response is better.<BR>In drag races, quick shifts make a big difference.  The RX-7 has much better shift linkage and feel.<BR>Traction.  Many RX-7s have limited slip differentials... they improve standing start acceleration a lot.<P>If you weren't thinking of drag racing, the same factors apply... I'd guess that the RX-7 is a sligtly faster road race car too.  Remember that both of these cars are at least 10 years old, however.  Stuff like tires, shocks, or basic engine maintenance could affect performance a lot too.||||reg||1
Z||000005||yobtah||12-10-2000||11:57 PM||||Here's a list of weights for '88 RX-7s:<P>2,625 lbs  (5 Speed) <BR>2,695 lbs  (Automatic) <BR>2,645 lbs  (5 Speed with 2+2) <BR>2,715 lbs  (Automatic with 2+2) <BR>2,845 lbs (TurboII)  <BR>3,003 lbs (Convertible) <P>Normally aspirated engines make 146hp@6500 and 138lb-ft@3500.<BR>Turbo IIs make 182hp@6500 and 183 lb-ft@3500.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Stoic||12-11-2000||08:19 AM||||Yeah, I was thinkin of drag racing him, but considering the current weather conditions and the problems I have recently encountered with my car, it has been put off, thats why I was wondering what kind of chance I'd have.  I was looking at some info on the rotary engine, being as I was not too farmiliar with it.  Very strange.  Seems like a very practicle design though.  I guess once my problems and the weather subsides I'll find out ||||reg||6
